  "comment": "@spec ACTION-EXECUTOR-6 @spec ACTION-EXECUTOR-24. The worker's `execute` client and the runner's `/v1/execute` route ship in different images, as do the writer and reader of the mode variable, so the whole route is frozen here. `mode_variable` is the runner-private boot variable (outside `BootEnv`) the worker sets on an executor claim. Every request carries exactly `request_keys`; a key a phase does not use is null. `phases` gives one request and response per phase; `arguments` is the canonical text (action-canonical-arguments.json), never an object. `sequences` lists the only accepted phase orders within one sandbox (a prefix of one is accepted; any other order, including a second `call`, is refused `phase_out_of_order` without dialing). `refusals` is the closed set of route refusals: HTTP status, body, whether the connector may have been dialed, and the pre-dispatch code the worker reports for it. `refused_tool_sequences` are orders refused for a given `call` tool (a forward tool never follows `observe`; a restore always does). `refused_call_consumes`: a `call` refused by preflight still spends the sandbox's one call, so a later `call` is refused without dialing. `connector_not_hosted` refuses a `call` whose connector the grant header cannot ride to (no URL). `bounds`: `list` pagination past `list_pages` is refused, and a `call` result past `call_result_bytes` is answered as an error with no structure (the worker fails it; it is never retried). `absent_route` is a runner image without the route. `status_body` is the executor mode body of `GET /status` and `GET /v1/status`; `other_routes` are refused in executor mode. Read by apps/worker/tests/test_runner_execute_vector.py and runner/tests/test_runner_execute_vector.py, each of which rejects an unknown key.",
